{"title":"1978 IMO Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_August_2026)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remarks (added by pf02, August 2026)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1978_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>An international society has its members from six different countries. The list of members has 1978 names, numbered <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/c/2bc93937658aa218f42a9ecd6752c8103fd7d174.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1, 2, \\ldots, 1978$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"105\" height=\"17\" />. Prove that there is at least one member whose number is the sum of the numbers of two (not necessarily distinct) members from his own country.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1978_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose the contrary. Then there exists a partition of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/3/473dacd8e0e1d2757ccfba089ec118ae88ce3950.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2,3,\\ldots, 1978\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"140\" height=\"20\" /> into 6 difference-free subsets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/f/68f8ba678233e72086c97ee6c18b0943502aac82.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B,C,D,E,F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"126\" height=\"16\" />. A set S is difference-free if there are not <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/b/b4be9dc2161474fba6caeedab71bb9279a73fcfa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y,z \\in S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"82\" height=\"18\" /> such that x = y - z.\n</p><p>By the Pigeonhole principle, one of these subsets, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, has a minimum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/9/3491e8fbbf5c62162a71d516e0109c92903ddd46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left \\lfloor \\frac{1978}{6}\\right \\rfloor +1 = 330$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"146\" height=\"45\" /> members, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/8/b683d647cb75cc09adee43fa606c877a40e9fb78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1 &lt; a_2 &lt; \\ldots &lt; a_{330}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"160\" height=\"15\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is difference-free, each of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/d/c9d8d8248691d20760796ba82fdb220845021636.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$329$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> differences <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/d/f7de598f04eb8a631c261c3e3de7c605f6ccf810.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{330} - a_1, a_{330} - a_2, \\ldots, a_{330} - a_{329}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"272\" height=\"16\" /> cannot belong to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, and so must belong to one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/4/564e8c516857de572a9b249c929de620719ef1dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B, C, D, E, F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"106\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>One of these subsets, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, has a minimum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/5/2e5e8dbd8eed6714d871ac45a80249ff09c06711.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left \\lfloor \\frac{329}{5}\\right \\rfloor +1 = 66$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"128\" height=\"45\" /> members, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0dc266dc8a20ded22028f1e50a3085e44df377b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_1 &lt; b_2 &lt; \\ldots &lt; b_{66}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"148\" height=\"17\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are difference-free, each of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/9/339baf6df71777af266e6259546917b0742caf2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$65$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> differences <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/a/daa9a8748d72a817146993929481b51baecc8ed1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_{66} - b_1, b_{66} - b_2, \\ldots, b_{66} - b_{65}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"235\" height=\"18\" /> cannot belong to one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/f/bef747a48a2a8d43c4841e12c89f2de2502a672e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"35\" height=\"16\" />, and so must belong to one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/3/00372dc27784ca23d89596bc4f1a6e20a0380452.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C, D, E, F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>One of these subsets, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, has a minimum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/0/b20e217cccfd914a2fcc8665ed3e0a54f65ee37a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left \\lfloor \\frac{65}{4}\\right \\rfloor +1 = 17$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"118\" height=\"45\" /> members, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/4/924fee528ea6603395398870572538a33e3c252a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_1 &lt; c_2 &lt; \\ldots &lt; c_{17}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"148\" height=\"15\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/299ddf7c0f03ea46bf09f81f93adeea0756aa15c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"58\" height=\"16\" /> are difference-free, each of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/5/9a5b4928c8fe50ce3c2428da3bee3505e891b788.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> differences <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/4/234045f27d7ac24765111263d24dc3ac7d60d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_{17} - c_1, c_{17} - c_2, \\ldots, c_{17} - c_{16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"235\" height=\"16\" /> cannot belong to one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/299ddf7c0f03ea46bf09f81f93adeea0756aa15c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"58\" height=\"16\" />, and so must belong to one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/8/c18e240b7c7dbc4b2e6da70cd13ba155dc475c0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D, E, F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"60\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>One of these subsets, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, has a minimum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/b/22bd4ae3df11994e3222ddde9c1c9122b618710e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left \\lfloor \\frac{16}{3}\\right \\rfloor +1 = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"109\" height=\"45\" /> members, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/4/da45f2a3dcb41785d31a25e8a444bfdc3b441bfc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d_1 &lt; d_2 &lt; \\ldots &lt; d_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"147\" height=\"17\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/7/0a70ef3ff6592247a7bb65ba8bb54d724b112890.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C, D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"18\" /> are difference-free, each of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> differences <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/9/e492f300292de00865bf112630ba72f392201ef3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d_6 - d_1, d_6 - d_2, \\ldots, d_6 - d_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"218\" height=\"18\" /> cannot belong to one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/7/0a70ef3ff6592247a7bb65ba8bb54d724b112890.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C, D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"18\" />, and so must belong to one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/3/033ff41b019f4360f1312fc129c262d1050e63c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E, F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"36\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>One of these subsets, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, has a minimum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/1/591878a80e0639771de647f60a4acd535108251b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left \\lfloor \\frac{5}{2}\\right \\rfloor +1 = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"100\" height=\"45\" /> members, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/f/60f6ede35ad033940b34b88f96f24cf714a69063.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_1 &lt; e_2 &lt; e_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"15\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/5/63568d5821ffdfe52fbdbe448e607084e73f9457.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C, D, E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"103\" height=\"16\" /> are difference-free, each of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> differences <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/d/60d20313809db8923895cdd19a9136d0e2899044.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_3 - e_1, e_3 - e_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"118\" height=\"16\" /> cannot belong to one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/5/63568d5821ffdfe52fbdbe448e607084e73f9457.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C, D, E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"103\" height=\"16\" />, and so must belong to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> has at least two members, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/a/cea33c9673cdf0e24c254583d774516f604611d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_1 &lt; f_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" />. The difference <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/1/7a1fa7129bc27bc1b7fad1bbdc30ce2f378df7a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g = f_2 - f_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" /> cannot belong to one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/e/e0e530ca11be5fa91e36be4b2e22182215ca2a1b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C, D, E, F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"126\" height=\"16\" />, a contradiction! <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Remarks_.28added_by_pf02.2C_August_2026.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_August_2026)\">Remarks (added by pf02, August 2026)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1978_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Remarks (added by pf02, August 2026)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/9/3299a521e2fdf8c25f985b77785afdf36a62c372.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{1:}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" /> The problem tries to be \"cute\" by using the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/7/967eac36d2d8bbd6ff14f1c5e40d154d4a7e93d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1978$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" />, but\na mathematician would ask: \"why exactly 1978?\" It is very easy to check that\nin fact, replacing 1978 by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/5/ae56d4474d69da5412dabe974b46a6635c730786.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1957$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> yields the same conclusion, using this proof. \n</p><p>A mathematician would ask then: Is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/5/ae56d4474d69da5412dabe974b46a6635c730786.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1957$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> the smallest number which forces\nthat there is at least one member whose number is the sum of the numbers of\ntwo (not necessarily distinct) members from his own country?\n</p><p>The answer is no, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/5/ae56d4474d69da5412dabe974b46a6635c730786.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1957$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> is probably not the smallest such number. This is\nalready suggested by the fact that if we use the argument in this proof, the\nsmallest number of members would seem be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> for two countries, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/5/9a5b4928c8fe50ce3c2428da3bee3505e891b788.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> for three\ncountries, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/9/339baf6df71777af266e6259546917b0742caf2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$65$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> for four countries. But a direct check, will yield that in\nfact, the smallest number of members in the society such that there is at least\none member whose number is the sum of the numbers of two (not necessarily\ndistinct) members from his own country is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/b/dcb86613295fa5cd7767aeae03da6b8ffab9ba56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5, 14, 45$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"17\" /> for two, three, four\ncountries, respectively.\n</p><p>The proof that for two countries the number is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is easy; the proof that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/0/820a20de2847dfdc1bc5cffc8d904933150032bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />\nis the number for three countries is not too difficult; the proof that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/7/f27da4fca6f3fecb215974023aad210b70ec3857.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> is\nthe number for four countries is more tedious, but it can be done with the help\nof a computer using some non-trivial programing.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/7/8e7127c7fbfcf4c508b325e93ddb81d79095785b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{2:}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" /> Let us re-formulate the problem: \n</p><p>For positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/f/3cff91106986d596b444b21427ff9ee5ac08db4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n, k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" />, consider partitions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/c/dac972879ad971e0ff92722f40c6dd0f6e535a3d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{A_1, \\dots, A_k\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"103\" height=\"20\" /> of\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/9/7899362421a645cc48294571ebf30af7bbc73b47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 2, \\dots, n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" />. A set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2744600189f7f1df31756f2022b2d5212ef1c767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> is called sum-free, if there is no <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/c/55c4de214f56a8317d2e746ff267d7884397b5be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y \\in A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"71\" height=\"18\" />\nsuch that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/c/37c4003b355ed3962c56025b6b1c2a25b31deb6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + y \\in A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"85\" height=\"18\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/5/32581661cddf01ad30c1e36efe72f99eb0a0adca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" /> could be equal). A partition is sum-free, if all\nthe sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2744600189f7f1df31756f2022b2d5212ef1c767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> are sum-free. For given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />, find the largest <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such that there is\na sum-free partition of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/9/7899362421a645cc48294571ebf30af7bbc73b47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 2, \\dots, n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>The number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is called the Schur number for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />, and it is denoted by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/b/74b9d8caa32e164594e695c19fece20f45b8c6b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" />.\n(Sometimes, people refer to the Schur number as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/9/dd9b8939fa09c5068b88e5f757670f6a2c74ce41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(k) + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"70\" height=\"20\" />, the smallest number\nsuch that there is no sum-free partition of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/9/7899362421a645cc48294571ebf30af7bbc73b47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 2, \\dots, n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>In the previous section, I stated that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/e/83ee523d03bbd117643547cff118e85fdfc4ac6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(2) = 4, S(3) = 13, S(4) = 44$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"242\" height=\"20\" />, and\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/9/4093be14e6747e3cccbc7c4ac56886fc33443a4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(6) \\le 1956$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"98\" height=\"20\" />. It turns out that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/b/0bb584461f5fefb39b8a6cc5606975f223e772be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(5) = 160$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"89\" height=\"20\" />, which is quite difficult to\nprove, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/2/152d60fa4ca5b2d34072502cd226bcb2783797e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" /> is unknown at this time.\n</p><p>For much more information see\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://reachthehorizon.com/problem/schur\">https://reachthehorizon.com/problem/schur</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://mathworld.wolfram.com/SchurNumber.html\">https://mathworld.wolfram.com/SchurNumber.html</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schur&#39;s_theorem\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schur&#39;s_theorem</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1978_IMO\" title=\"1978 IMO\">1978 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1978_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1978 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1978_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1978 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1978_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1978 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1978_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1978 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1978_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1978 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1978_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1978 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1978_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1978 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b>Last Question</b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017165422\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.035 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.051 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 381/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 867/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 51/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 15176/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.805      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    6.805      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:15660:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017165422 and revision id 289979. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}